== Directions for Giving

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I told you#super[+] before that I am willing to do you a kindness. And so that it may be a kindness indeed, I must tell you#super[+] further not only what you should do, but #emph[how you must do it, so that you may not lose your reward.] And if you want to make sure of being gainers by whatever you lay out in this way, observe the following #emph[DIRECTIONS.]]

#emph[I.] Give yourselves to the Lord, and with yourselves all that you have, to be laid out, whether on yourselves or on others, as he appoints and requires.

2 Cor 8:2. The #emph[apostle,] boasting of the #emph[Macedonian Christians] and of the riches of their liberality, tells us in v. 5 #emph[that they gave themselves first to the Lord.] The man who will not give #emph[himself] to the Lord is likely to give little else. And if he should give all that he had, and withhold only himself, God will not accept it or reward it. God will have nothing of you if he may not have your heart.

Give yourselves to the Lord as #emph[servants,] to serve him with your#super[+] spirits first, and then with all that you have. Make yourselves over to God in Christ to be his #emph[covenant servants,] and from then on count and say of yourselves, as the #emph[apostle] teaches you#super[+] (1 Cor 6:20), #emph[I am not my own, but must from now on glorify God with my body and my spirit, yes, and with all that I have, which are his.]

The man who says #emph[I serve the Lord,] and has not first given himself to him as a servant, does not know what he is saying; God will not be served by aliens. And the man who says #emph[I give myself to the Lord as a servant,] and does not actually serve him with all that he has, has only lied to God. The man who has given #emph[himself] to the Lord will withhold nothing from him that God will have.

The man who will serve the Lord with his spirit will just as readily serve him with all that he has. God, who has given us his #emph[Son,] “how will He not also, along with Him, freely give us all things?” (Rom 8:32). And the man who has given God his soul will keep nothing back from him.

Friend, you say you are the #emph[Lord’s] and have devoted yourself to him. You hope that you are Christ’s, and have joined yourself to him in covenant, resolving to be his disciple. But which way does your estate run? Whose is this house, these lands, and this money that you have? Are these the Lord’s too? How is it, then, that your #emph[lusts] have so much of them at their service? That your #emph[pride] has so much, and your appetite so much? Or that they are all taken over and kept by you to feed your #emph[covetousness?] Is all you have the #emph[Lord’s?] How is it, then, that you begrudge every little thing he calls for from you, or think it too much? You who will not, in every case, part with as much as God calls for, are either a liar or a robber. Either you did not give all to God when you said you did, and then you are a #emph[liar;] or if you did give all, and yet will not let him have what he calls for but bestow it elsewhere, then you are a #emph[robber.]

#emph[Obj.] But some will say, “Must I give all I have to the poor, and so leave myself and my family to be beggars? Or what else do you mean when you say I must give all to the Lord?”

My meaning is this: #emph[that you so give all to the Lord as to resolve to dispose of your whole estate to such persons and purposes as God orders and appoints for you.] The Lord would have you live and maintain yourself, and provide for your family soberly, according to your rank and station (some extraordinary cases excepted). But still you must allow no more to yourself, and no less to those in need, than accords with the will and good pleasure of the Lord.

Someone else will put in here and say, “But I am free and bountiful, and give great alms. I cast my bread upon the waters; I give a portion to six, and also to seven. I devise liberal things; I delight to show mercy.”

Do you? It is well that you do; God bless your heart for it. It would be a great pity for any liberal man in the world to lose his reward. And so that you may not lose yours, take this counsel from a friend who loves you. See that you have sincerely given yourself up to God as his #emph[covenant servant,] and are a devoted disciple of Jesus Christ. See that these works of mercy of yours are done in keeping with your covenant, as a part of the service that you owe and have vowed to your Lord, whose you are, and whose talents you count all that you have. And look to this all the more because it is possible that men of great good works may be men of little charity, and of little faith also. And faith is the grace that gives God his right to us and to all we do, and obtains our acceptance with him. First by faith give yourself to him, and then by charity serve him with what you have, and then do not doubt of a plentiful return.

II. #emph[Offer up your#super[+] gift on the altar.] Give yourselves, and with yourselves all that you have, #emph[through Christ,] to God. Let him be #emph[your#super[+] altar that sanctifies your gift.] It is through him alone that you will be accepted: “He has made us accepted in the Beloved One” (Eph 1:6). Let your#super[+] #emph[souls be sprinkled with his blood,] and so washed from your blood, from your natural and acquired guilt and pollution; and then they will be a gift acceptable to the Lord. Do not think you#super[+] have nothing more to do than to give yourselves to the Lord as you are. You#super[+] have given yourselves to sin, and now perhaps you think you will give yourselves back to the Lord; but know that there is more to it than that. You are in yourself a guilty and defiled soul, and God will have none of you in this state. Go to #emph[Christ] first, and get him to cover your unrighteousness and to cleanse you from your filthiness. Put your wretched soul into his hands, and let him present it to the Father.

And whatever you give along with yourself for his poor, #emph[your alms, your bread,] or #emph[your flesh,] or #emph[your money,] put it all into the same hand, and let him present this also to the Father, for the use of his servants.

Friends, though I would have you#super[+] be givers, yet I would not have you be losers. I would not have you#super[+] be losers either by #emph[saving] or by #emph[unprofitable giving.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Do not be losers by saving.] That is the quickest way to #emph[lose all:] thinking to #emph[save and keep all to yourselves.] “Whoever wants to save his life” (that is, by not laying it down when God calls for it) “will lose it.” And so #emph[whoever wants to save his estate,] that is, #emph[by refusing to lay it out where] God would have him, #emph[is all the more likely to lose all that he has.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Do not lose by unprofitable giving.] Everything is given unprofitably, as far as you are concerned, whoever you give it to, if it is not given first through Christ to God, and to his servants for the Lord’s sake. It may still be a profit to those who receive it, but it is no profit to you who give. It is only what is given to God, and to men for God’s sake, for which God becomes a debtor.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[III. #emph[Fetch all your#super[+] alms out of your hearts.] “Draw out your soul to the hungry” (Isa 58:10). Give what you give 1, #emph[out of a willing heart;] 2, #emph[out of a compassionate heart;] 3, #emph[out of a thankful heart.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Out of a willing heart; give willingly.] 2 Cor 9:7: “Each one should give what he has decided in his heart to give, not out of regret or compulsion. For God loves a cheerful giver.” An alms-deed is called in Scripture a #emph[sacrifice,] a sacrifice that is well pleasing to God (Heb 13:16): “Do not neglect to do good and to share with others, for with such sacrifices God is pleased.” Now all our Christian sacrifices are to be #emph[freewill offerings.] An alms without a will is a sacrifice without a heart, and will be rejected by God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Out of a compassionate heart.] Get a compassionate heart, and fetch all your alms out of your own heart. #emph[It is the hard hearts of men] that shut up their #emph[purses.] You say you have nothing to spare: you need it yourself, or those around you may need it. #emph[No, you lack a heart,] and that is the reason those in need must go without #emph[your alms.] A compassionate heart would find something or other for those who are in distress.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Get a compassionate heart. God is a God of compassion, and his children are compassionate children. Col 3:12: “As the elect of God, … clothe yourselves with hearts of compassion.” 1 John 3:17: “If anyone with earthly possessions sees his brother in need, but withholds his compassion from him” (or is a man without compassion), “how can the love of God abide in him?” Whatever your profession or your religion may be, or your hopes concerning it, you have nothing of God in you; you are none of his, if you have no compassion. God is a God of mercy; his children are all merciful children.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Worldling, will you prove yourself to be #emph[no child of God?] Would you prove that the #emph[faith] you have is #emph[vain,] your #emph[religion vain,] and that you are only #emph[a pretender to the love of God,] and do not have the love of God within you? This unmercifulness of yours will put it beyond question. If you have no compassion, the love of God is not in you.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[O get a compassionate and merciful heart. Have you received mercy, and will you not be merciful? Do you hope for mercy, and will you not show mercy? Put yourself in your poor brother’s place for a while. Think how hard it is with him; think of #emph[his hunger] and #emph[nakedness,] of #emph[his pressing straits and miseries.] Let your soul go into his house and see #emph[his bare walls, his cold fireplace, his empty cupboard, his starving children.] And then think again: O what if it were like this with me! Ah, poor distressed creatures, how they are #emph[pinched] and #emph[pined,] while I have enough and abound! O my #emph[compassion,] how can you not yearn? O my #emph[heart,] how can you not bleed over such distressed ones? Have you nothing to help them? There is enough in my house, there is enough in my purse to give them relief; but is there no alms for them in my heart? Can I have the heart to see them pine and perish, and do nothing to help them? Where are you, O my pity? Where are my compassions? O my soul, help, and send portions to those for whom nothing is provided.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. #emph[Out of a thankful heart.] Remember what God has done for you. Have you any sense or experience of his special love to you in Christ? What should that produce? The sense of divine goodness in the way of #emph[common providence] calls for all due acknowledgment from us. But a #emph[taste] of his #emph[fatherly love in pardoning our sins, justifying our persons, and bringing us into the possession of eternal life and happiness, on account of his Son’s suffering and dying for us,] is both the strongest #emph[motive] to our charity, or kindness, to our fellow creatures who stand in need of it, and the most #emph[commanding reason] for it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[In Christ the beams of God’s mercy are gathered to a point, as the beams of the sun are in a burning glass. As they fall on our hearts, our hearts are, or should be, set on fire by them, and made to burn with a greater heat of love and kindness to all in want, especially to all who bear his image. #emph[The right spring of mercy to our neighbor is the sense of God’s mercy toward us.]]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[IV. #emph[Let all your#super[+] streams of love flow into the ocean.] My meaning is, #emph[let all be done to the glory of God.] This must be your last end, to which all must be directed, as the apostle charges in 1 Cor 10:31: “Whether you eat or drink” (I may add, or whether you give to eat or to drink) “or whatever you do, do it all to the glory of God.” Let this be chiefly in your eye and your heart: #emph[that God may be glorified.] There are other, subordinate ends, such as #emph[the refreshing of the needy,] the #emph[adorning of our holy profession,] and the #emph[edification of others by our good example;] but all must at last end here, #emph[that God may be glorified.]]

Take care that your end is not to glorify yourselves. As #emph[worms] breed in the fairest fruit, so #emph[pride] and #emph[vainglory] are apt to creep up out of the best duties.

V. #emph[Let your#super[+] works of charity be done in humility, giving God the honor of them, by acknowledging that what you have bestowed on the poor you first received from him, and that it was his goodness that gave you a heart to give anything out of your abundance toward their relief.] Indeed, you ought always to keep a jealous watch over yourselves, in case there has been some #emph[hypocrisy] and #emph[self-seeking] in your works of #emph[charity.] Acknowledge that you are so far from meriting #emph[heaven] and #emph[salvation by your good works] that, if God should deal with you according to the rigor of his justice, he might cast you into #emph[hell] for the #emph[pride] and #emph[hypocrisy] that cling to your best works.

VI. #emph[Let your#super[+] works of charity to men be accompanied with prayers and thanksgivings to God.] Thank God that he has put you among the #emph[givers] and not among the #emph[receivers,] since “it is more blessed to give than to receive”; that he has put you among the #emph[givers] and not the #emph[withholders;] that he has given you an #emph[estate] to give and a #emph[heart] to give. Thank God that he will accept a gift at your#super[+] hands: that he, to whom all you have belongs, and to whom it is owed, will count as a gift what is only the payment of so small a part of your debt. Say with #emph[David] in 1 Chronicles 29:11, 13: “Yours, O #smallcaps[Lord], is the greatness … Both riches and honor come from You … Now therefore, our God, we give You thanks, and we praise Your glorious name. But who am I, and who are my people, that we should be able to give as generously as this? For everything comes from You, and from Your own hand we have given to You.”

VII. #emph[Give out in proportion to what God has given to you#super[+],] 1 Cor 16:2. The apostle advises the #emph[Corinthians] to give #emph[as God had prospered them.]

Rich men, therefore, ought to be #emph[rich in good works,] for God expects fruit to match the seed that he sows. Has he #emph[abounded] toward you#super[+] in this world’s goods? Then you#super[+] ought to be #emph[abundant] in good works toward others. Your#super[+] pounds are #emph[expected,] where the widow’s mites are #emph[accepted.]
